\draw[thick] (A)--(B) node[midway,below] {base};\draw (B)--(C) node[midway,right] {$x$ (slant)};\draw (C)--(D);\draw (D)--(A) node[midway,left] {$y$ (slant)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
The same triangle is shown twice. In diagram $1$, $AB$ is the base. In diagram $2$, $AC$ is the base. Must the matching height be the same segment in both diagrams?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A1) at (0,0);\coordinate (B1) at (3.2,0);\coordinate (C1) at (0.9,2.4);\draw (A1)--(B1)--(C1)--cycle;\node[below] at (1.6,0) {$AB$};\node at (1.6,-0.8) {$1$};
\coordinate (A2) at (5,0);\coordinate (B2) at (8.2,0);\coordinate (C2) at (5.9,2.4);\draw (A2)--(B2)--(C2)--cycle;\node[left] at (5.45,1.2) {$AC$};\node at (6.6,-0.8) {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.solution_text:
No. The height must be perpendicular to the chosen base.
Changing the base from $AB$ to $AC$ changes the direction and position of its matching height.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A1) at (0,0);\coordinate (B1) at (3.2,0);\coordinate (C1) at (0.9,2.4);\coordinate (H1) at (0.9,0);\draw[thick] (A1)--(B1) node[midway,below] {base $AB$};\draw (B1)--(C1)--(A1);\draw[thick,dashed] (C1)--(H1);\coordinate (r1a) at ($(H1)!0.25cm!(C1)$);\coordinate (r1b) at ($(H1)!0.25cm!(B1)$);\draw (r1a)--($(r1a)+(r1b)-(H1)$)--(r1b);
\coordinate (A2) at (5,0);\coordinate (B2) at (8.2,0);\coordinate (C2) at (5.9,2.4);\coordinate (H2) at (5.395,1.052);\draw (A2)--(B2)--(C2);\draw[thick] (C2)--(A2) node[midway,left] {base $AC$};\draw[thick,dashed] (B2)--(H2);\coordinate (r2a) at ($(H2)!0.25cm!(B2)$);\coordinate (r2b) at ($(H2)!0.25cm!(A2)$);\draw (r2a)--($(r2a)+(r2b)-(H2)$)--(r2b);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
A student chooses the top side of a parallelogram as the base. Where can its matching perpendicular height end?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);\coordinate (B) at (4.5,0);\coordinate (C) at (5.8,2.5);\coordinate (D) at (1.3,2.5);\draw (A)--(B)--(C);\draw[thick] (C)--(D) node[midway,above] {base};\draw (D)--(A);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.solution_text:
It can end anywhere on the opposite bottom side as long as the segment meets both parallel sides at $90^{\circ}$.
Its length is the perpendicular distance between the parallel sides.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);\coordinate (B) at (4.5,0);\coordinate (C) at (5.8,2.5);\coordinate (D) at (1.3,2.5);\coordinate (H) at (1.3,0);\draw (A)--(B)--(C);\draw[thick] (C)--(D) node[midway,above] {base};\draw (D)--(A);\draw[thick,dashed] (D)--(H) node[midway,right] {height};\coordinate (ra) at ($(D)!0.3cm!(C)$);\coordinate (rb) at ($(D)!0.3cm!(H)$);\draw (ra)--($(ra)+(rb)-(D)$)--(rb);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

